IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wk-nouns`
IMPORT `wk-agreements`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for Business Activities Affecting Workers (2025), section 3:
-- sharing competitively sensitive information with competitors.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW (n.1 [pdf page 2]). Each result is a sentence of
-- section 3 with its own modal ("may violate", "may be unlawful", "can be unlawful");
-- none says that an exchange was lawful or unlawful. EMPTY and NOTHING as in
-- wk-agreements.l4 (fork F23).

§ `Section 3: sharing competitively sensitive information`

DECLARE `What section 3 says of the exchange` IS ONE OF
    `sharing competitively sensitive information with competitors about terms and conditions of employment may violate the antitrust laws`
    `exchanging competitively sensitive compensation or other employment information with a competitor may be unlawful when the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended: such an effect is found`
    `exchanging competitively sensitive compensation or other employment information with a competitor may be unlawful when the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended: its effect has not been assessed`
    `information exchanges may indicate the existence of a wage-fixing conspiracy`
    `providing competitively sensitive information through an algorithm or through a third party's tool or product may also be unlawful`
    `information exchanges facilitated by or through a third party, including through an algorithm or other software, that are used to generate wage or other benefit recommendations can be unlawful even if the exchange does not require businesses to strictly adhere to those recommendations`
    `an agreement to use shared wage recommendations, lists, calculations, or algorithms can also still be unlawful even where co-conspirators retain some discretion or cheat on the agreement`
    `even if the companies are parties to a legitimate transaction or a joint venture or other collaborative activity, an agreement between them to share information about wages or other terms of employment may violate the antitrust laws`

-- 3 [pdf pages 7-8], in the text's order:
--   * "Sharing competitively sensitive information with competitors about terms and
--     conditions of employment may violate the antitrust laws." (n.22)
--   * "may be unlawful when the information exchange has, or is likely to have, an
--     anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended" (n.23): given in its
--     applied form when the effect is found, in its pending form when the effect has not
--     been assessed, and not at all when the contrary is found (fork F12). Intent is not
--     read.
--   * "information exchanges may indicate the existence of a wage-fixing conspiracy",
--     read with the competitively sensitive exchanges of the same paragraph (fork F13).
--   * "Providing competitively sensitive information through an algorithm or through a
--     third party's tool or product may also be unlawful." Read for any route through a
--     third party, because the text's own example of it (n.24, the poultry processors) is
--     an exchange "through a third-party firm that facilitated the exchange" (fork F25).
--     That example is a fixture.
--   * Exchanges "facilitated by or through a third party (including through an algorithm
--     or other software) that are used to generate wage or other benefit
--     recommendations" (n.25): whether adherence is required is not read.
--   * "An agreement to use shared wage recommendations, lists, calculations, or
--     algorithms can also still be unlawful even where co-conspirators retain some
--     discretion or cheat on the agreement": discretion and cheating are not read.
--   * "Even if companies are parties to a legitimate transaction or are otherwise involved
--     in a joint venture or other collaborative activity, an agreement between the
--     companies to share information about wages or other terms of employment ... may
--     violate the antitrust laws": the transaction is not read.
-- Every sentence is about exchanges with competitors (the section's heading; Overview
-- item 3 [pdf page 3], "companies that compete for workers"), so the whole section is read only for
-- businesses that compete for workers in the same labor market: EMPTY for potentially
-- competing employers and for businesses that do not compete, NOTHING when the caller has
-- not said (fork F13). The sentences that say "competitively sensitive" need that field;
-- the last three do not use the words and do not read it. Whether the exchange is
-- explicit or implied is not read (3 [pdf page 7]).
@ref 3 [pdf pages 7-8]; n.22-n.25 [pdf page 7]; Overview, item 3 [pdf page 3]
GIVEN x IS A `Information exchange`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `What section 3 says of the exchange`)
`3: what section 3 says of the exchange` MEANS
    CONSIDER `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (x's `the businesses`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST c THEN
        IF c EQUALS `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
        THEN JUST (catMaybes (LIST (IF sensitive THEN JUST `sharing competitively sensitive information with competitors about terms and conditions of employment may violate the antitrust laws` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF sensitive AND effect EQUALS `the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect` THEN JUST `exchanging competitively sensitive compensation or other employment information with a competitor may be unlawful when the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended: such an effect is found` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF sensitive AND effect EQUALS `the exchange's effect has not been assessed` THEN JUST `exchanging competitively sensitive compensation or other employment information with a competitor may be unlawful when the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended: its effect has not been assessed` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF sensitive THEN JUST `information exchanges may indicate the existence of a wage-fixing conspiracy` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF sensitive AND (NOT direct) THEN JUST `providing competitively sensitive information through an algorithm or through a third party's tool or product may also be unlawful` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF (NOT direct) AND x's `the exchange is used to generate wage or other benefit recommendations` THEN JUST `information exchanges facilitated by or through a third party, including through an algorithm or other software, that are used to generate wage or other benefit recommendations can be unlawful even if the exchange does not require businesses to strictly adhere to those recommendations` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF x's `the businesses agree to use shared wage recommendations, lists, calculations, or algorithms` THEN JUST `an agreement to use shared wage recommendations, lists, calculations, or algorithms can also still be unlawful even where co-conspirators retain some discretion or cheat on the agreement` ELSE NOTHING),
                                   (IF x's `the businesses agree to share information about wages or other terms of employment` THEN JUST `even if the companies are parties to a legitimate transaction or a joint venture or other collaborative activity, an agreement between them to share information about wages or other terms of employment may violate the antitrust laws` ELSE NOTHING)))
        ELSE JUST EMPTY
  WHERE
    sensitive MEANS x's `the information is competitively sensitive information about compensation or other terms or conditions of employment`
    effect MEANS x's `the finding on the exchange's effect`
    direct MEANS x's `how the information is shared` EQUALS `directly between the businesses`
